export default {"id":"djing-level-2","number":2,"title":"Musical Time for DJs","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"Effective mixes start the incoming track so that its phrase or section changes coincide with those of the outgoing track (How-to-DJ phrasing rule). Launches mid-phrase produce discontinuities that remain audible even when tempos match. The ","modules":[{"id":"djing-m-2-1","code":"2","title":"Musical Time for DJs","blurb":"Effective mixes start the incoming track so that its phrase or section changes coincide with those of the outgoing track (How-to-DJ phrasing rule). Launches mid-phrase produce discontinuities that remain audible even when tempos match. The ","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"djing-l-o-2","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 2 — Musical Time for DJs","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Objective:</strong> Identify musical structure so mix points are determined by form, not by chance.   <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate — two lessons requiring counting against commercial tracks.   <strong>Sources:</strong> <em>How-to-DJ</em> §2.2–3.1; Berklee public syllabus (song structure and phrase mixing); common instructional use of the 8-bar phrase as a frequent 4/4 unit, with genre qualifications."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"djing-l-2-1-1","title":"2.1.1 — Beats, Bars, Phrases, and Song Form","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Temporal hierarchy","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Unit","Typical 4/4 size","Operational question"],"rows":[["Beat","One pulse","Where is the kick grid?"],["Bar","Four beats","Where is beat 1 of the bar?"],["Phrase","Often 8 bars / 32 beats in dance 4/4","Where do producers add or remove elements?"],["Section","Intro, verse, build, drop, breakdown, outro (labels vary)","Where can a track enter or exit without disrupting formal continuity?"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Effective mixes start the incoming track so that its phrase or section changes coincide with those of the outgoing track (<em>How-to-DJ</em> phrasing rule). Launches mid-phrase produce discontinuities that remain audible even when tempos match."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Genre qualification","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The 8-bar model is most reliable for electronic and dance 4/4 material. Hip-hop, pop, and radio edits frequently use shorter intros (8 or 16 beats) or irregular arrangements. Phrase length is established by listening. Applying a fixed formula while ignoring the music is an error."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Song form and mix points","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Identify:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Exit regions</strong> — breakdowns, outros, filtered endings, and loop-compatible 8-bar units where the outgoing track can leave  ","<strong>Entry regions</strong> — intros, tools, and reduced openings where the incoming track can enter without concurrent full vocal lines  ","<strong>Drop on beat 1</strong> — simultaneous downbeat and section energy alignment of both tracks  ","<strong>Vocal conflict regions</strong> — two full vocal performances competing in the same midrange  "],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Selection is part of timing: a library composed only of peak sections provides few suitable join points."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure — phrase map of one track (approximately 15 minutes)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select one familiar 4/4 dance track.  ","Without relying on a waveform editor, count bars (1–2–3–4 per bar). Mark each phrase start (bar 1 of each 8-bar group) by clap or muted tap.  ","Write a map of 6–10 lines: intro length, first full groove, build, drop, breakdown, outro.  ","Mark two valid exit points and one mid-phrase location unsuitable for exit.  ","Repeat on a hip-hop or radio track and note differences in phrase length."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Common errors","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Treating every 8 bars as universal law across genres  ","Relying only on waveforms or phrase markers without internalizing the count  ","Confusing arrangement energy changes with tempo problems  "],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 2.1.1","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Phrase-map five tracks (dance 4/4 material plus at least one non-dance structure). For each track record: phrase length heard, best exit, least suitable mid-track leave. Time limit: 90 minutes total."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pass criteria:</strong> At least four of five maps have phrase boundaries confirmed by re-counting with eyes closed on phrase starts. Phrase starts can be clapped on an unfamiliar track within 16 bars of first listening."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"djing-l-2-2-1","title":"2.2.1 — Mix-Point Decision Laboratory","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.1 — Mix-Point Decision Laboratory","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Decision framework","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Before planning a blend, answer:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Tempo neighborhood</strong> — heuristic only: many instructors use approximately ±5–10 BPM as a beginner comfort range (<em>How-to-DJ</em>); this is not a law. Larger jumps require more reliable warps and a clearer musical rationale.  ","<strong>Density</strong> — can the two textures coexist for 8–32 bars?  ","<strong>Vocal or lead conflict</strong> — yes or no; if yes, cut or EQ, or choose a different entry.  ","<strong>Exit and entry regions</strong> — do both tracks offer a valid join window?  ","<strong>Energy intent</strong> — maintain, raise, or lower intensity? Unintended energy reversal is a frequent cause of weak sets."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"Energy contour: open → build → peak → cool","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Describe energy, not only BPM (<em>How-to-DJ</em> set arc). BPM may remain constant while energy changes through density, arrangement, and later key. Beginner contour:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Phase","Intent"],"rows":[["Open","Establish groove; leave room to increase intensity"],["Build","Raise intensity gradually"],["Peak","Highest planned intensity"],["Cool","Reduce intensity while remaining musical"]],"caption":null},{"type":"heading","text":"Procedure — six-track set skeleton (paper only)","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Choose six tracks with usage rights.  ","Assign each a role: opener, groover, builder, peak, cooler, floater.  ","For each adjacent pair, write: phrase join plan (8-bar, 16-bar, or cut), risks (vocal, density, tempo), and backup exit if the blend fails.  ","Identify one intentionally poor pair and explain why it fails (anti-pattern training)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"Laboratory 2.2.1","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Deliver a one-page set map for a 15-minute mini-set (to be performed after Gate A/C hardware requirements exist). Include energy arc labels, mix-point type per transition, and one contingency substitution."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pass criteria:</strong> Map shows a planned arc rather than random order; every transition has a named join strategy; at least one risk is mitigated (EQ plan, cut instead of blend, or reordered tracks)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Module 2 checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Phrase counting automatic on dance 4/4; genre qualifications applied  ","[ ] Exit and entry regions identified without exclusive reliance on screen colors  ","[ ] Six-track skeleton set map complete with risk notes  "],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
